The Grid is a wonderful opportunity!  We suffer three downchecks. 
First, we are awfully small and unimportant compared to their mission
statement.  Second, program speed is sensitive to OS and CPU - we or
the user custom compile.  Third, it consumes at least 98% of all CPU
cycles when running.  We've also looked at BOINC.

I've talked with my programmers.  Their opinion is that volunteered
unused or overnight CPUs make more sense.  It looks like we are OK
through 50,000 A radius.  That fills the gap by late this month worst
case,

http://www.mazepath.com/uncleal/qzdense.png

If I can find an idle server or three, we might make it to
75,000-100,000 A or so.  Every time we crunch out physicists'
criticisms they weasel out and say, "but..." We crunched out to 444
quadrillion atoms and 0.22 mm radius atom by atom.  WAS THAT ENOUGH! 
Nah... "What about the fine structure and Green's function?"   Up
their buts.  Do 25,000 points linear least squares fit a line?  50,000
points?  What will there be left to "but?"

-- 
